  "worked_solution": "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{\u2033}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-9x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+25{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-9\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+25{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-9\\lambda +25] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-9\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =5.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{5}+{c}_{2}{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n-2={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=4.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{\u2032}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =5{x}^{4}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}{x}^{4}+5{c}_{2}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=4$ and ${c}_{1}=-2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =5{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 4 & =-10+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =-2{x}^{5}+14{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}(7\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
